criteria.descriptionMarkdown:
Every own package (the 31 workspace members outside `vendor/`) is version `1.0.0`:

- `[workspace.package] version = "1.0.0"`; every own manifest keeps `version.workspace = true`;
- each of the 30 own `[workspace.dependencies]` entries (every own package except `gpux-testkit`, which nothing depends on) has `version = "1.0.0"`, and so does `crates/gpui_mobile/Cargo.toml`'s inline `gpui = { package = "gpux", path = "../gpui", version = ... }`; the path-only cycle-edge dev-dependencies stay versionless;
- the fork crates keep `30.0.1-gpux.1` and `0.27.1-gpux.1` with their `=`-pinned requirements, and no file under `vendor/` changes;
- `Cargo.lock` changes only in own packages' `version` lines and in `gpux-testkit`'s dependency list (its new dev-dependencies); no registry package is added, removed or changed.

As lasting invariants (they hold after every release PR too) the new group `tools/testkit/tests/release_config.rs` asserts: every own version requirement reached by a `path` outside `vendor/` (root `[workspace.dependencies]` and member manifests) equals the workspace version; the workspace version's major is 1 (the 1.x policy; the assertion changes when the policy ends); every fork package's version carries a `-gpux.` suffix and differs from it.

`release-plz.toml` at the repository root is the configuration the spike proved (d-release-tooling):

- `[workspace]`: `git_only = true`, `publish = false`, `git_tag_name = "v{{ version }}"`, `semver_check = false`, `git_tag_enable`, `git_release_enable` and `changelog_update` false, and `release_commits = "^(feat|fix|perf)"` (amendment 1: no breaking alternative; breaking work is typed `feat`);
- `gpux`: `version_group = "gpux"`, tag, GitHub Release and changelog on, `git_release_name = "v{{ version }}"` (d-release-tooling-proven), `changelog_path = "CHANGELOG.md"`, `changelog_include` = exactly the 30 other own packages;
- every other own package: `version_group = "gpux"` and nothing turned on; each of the 8 fork crates: `release = false`; every workspace package has exactly one `[[package]]` entry and no entry names a package outside the workspace;
- `[changelog]`: a header naming gpux and saying the whole workspace is released as one version, `## [{{ version }}] - {{ timestamp | date(format="%Y-%m-%d") }}` headings, RayX.CLI's Conventional-Commit groups, `chore(agentx)`, `chore(release)`, `chore(deps)` and merge commits skipped, `tag_pattern = "v[0-9].*"`, and a commit's `Migration:` footer (git-cliff `commit.footers`, token `Migration`) rendered as a nested `  - Migration: <note>` line under the commit (d-release-tooling-proven), as in the other library repositories (its rendering is proven in SPIKE-1's preview).

`release_config.rs` asserts all of it and asserts the body template iterates `commit.footers` and renders the `Migration` token, and evaluates `release_commits` with the `regex` crate (release-plz's engine): it matches `feat: x`, `fix(gpui): x`, `perf: x`, `feat(gpux): import Zed GPUI closure 0123abc` and does not match `docs: x`, `test: x`, `ci: x`, `chore: x`, `chore(agentx): x`, `refactor(gpui): x`, `build: x`, `style: x`. Where the spike proved a different value, the test asserts the proven value and the design records why.

`.github/actions/plan/plan.sh` for `EVENT_NAME=pull_request` (d-release-pr-ci), proven by the new group `tools/testkit/tests/ci_plan_release_pr.rs` in temporary git repositories:

1. no labels; changes = root `Cargo.toml` (`[workspace.package] version` and own path-dependency versions), a member `Cargo.toml` (an inline own path-dependency version), `Cargo.lock` (own `version` lines) and `CHANGELOG.md` -> `jobs=["check","package"]`, `os=["ubuntu-24.04"]`, `skipped` empty, and no `test`, `lane-a`, `lane-b`, `lane-c`, `lane-d`, `lane-e` or `lane-f`;
2. the same plus a lockfile registry bump (`version` and `checksum` lines) -> every job;
3. the same plus a non-version manifest change (a feature, a new dependency) -> every job;
4. a crates.io requirement change (`version` on a line without `path`) -> every job;
5. a fork requirement change (`path = "vendor/..."`) or any file under `vendor/` -> every job;
6. version-only manifests plus any other file (source, workflow) -> every job;
7. a release-shaped change with `ci:lane-a` -> `["lane-a"]`; with `ci-os:macos` -> `["check","package"]` on `["macos-15"]`;
8. `CHANGELOG.md` only -> `["check"]` (documentation-only rule).

The existing `ci_plan` group passes. The script reads the changed-file list whole (no pipe into `grep -q`) and works with GNU (ubuntu runners) and BSD (macOS) tools; its test is ignored on Windows with the reason, as `ci_plan.rs` does.

`docs/releasing.md` explains:

- how a release happens: Conventional Commits on `main`, release-plz's release PR (version bump of the whole workspace, `Cargo.lock`, the `CHANGELOG.md` section), merging it, then the `v` tag and the GitHub Release; one version and one tag for the whole workspace; the fork crates are never released;
- the 1.x policy and its commit-type table: `fix`/`perf` patch, `feat` minor; no `!` and no `BREAKING CHANGE:` footer while the owner is the only consumer; breaking work, every Zed import that breaks the API included (`feat(gpux): import Zed GPUI closure <sha>`), is `feat` (or `fix`) with the break stated in the body and consumer (RayX) notes in a `Migration:` footer, which the changelog shows under the commit; `docs`/`test`/`ci`/`chore`/`refactor`/`build` ride along in the next release; the policy ends when a second consumer appears, and then `!` and major bumps return;
- before merging a release PR: its CI (`check` on ubuntu, `package`) is green, the latest nightly run on `main` is green (the released commits were pushed straight to `main` without pull-request CI), it changes only `Cargo.toml` files, `Cargo.lock` and `CHANGELOG.md`, and the proposed version is still 1.x; a 2.0.0 proposal means a stray `!` or footer: reword that commit (force push allowed) instead of merging;
- `RELEASE_PLZ_TOKEN`: the repository secret holding the fine-grained PAT shared with RayX.CLI (Contents and Pull requests read/write), that its repository access must include Gpux (otherwise release-plz fails at checkout or push), why the default `GITHUB_TOKEN` does not work, and the silent-expiry failure mode (red Release-plz runs, no release PR, no tag) and rotation;
- the by-hand fallback for Gpux: set `[workspace.package] version` and every own requirement (`[workspace.dependencies]` and `crates/gpui_mobile`'s inline one), `cargo update --workspace --offline`, add the `## [X.Y.Z] - YYYY-MM-DD` section, push with the push policy, `git tag vX.Y.Z`, push the tag, `gh release create vX.Y.Z` with the section as notes;
- the "Release tags" ruleset (`refs/tags/v*`: `update`, `deletion`, `non_fast_forward`, no bypass; tags never move or disappear, creation stays allowed) and the push policy (`git fetch origin && git rebase origin/main && git push --force-with-lease origin main`; implementation never goes through pull requests);
- a new workspace crate (a Zed import) joins `release-plz.toml`'s `gpux` version group and gpux's `changelog_include` (`release_config.rs` fails until it does);
- how the configuration was proven (the spike's scenarios, the release-plz version, the observations such as root-only or `vendor/`-only commits) and how to rehearse a configuration change in a scratch clone with a local bare remote.

The README links it. Verified by review.

Verify-only (the owner set these up on 2026-10-18; nothing is created), recorded in the task handoff before the push:

1. `gh secret list --repo rayx-ui/Gpux` lists `RELEASE_PLZ_TOKEN` (its PAT's repository access cannot be read; FIRST-1 handles a missing grant);
2. the ruleset "Release tags" is active: `gh api repos/rayx-ui/Gpux/rulesets` and its detail show target `tag`, include `refs/tags/v*`, rules `update`, `deletion` and `non_fast_forward`, no bypass actors and no `creation` rule;
3. the rayx release that contains `rayx-cli-library-agnostic` (expected 1.4.0) is released: `gh release view v<version> --repo rayx-ui/RayX.CLI` and its tag contains that epic's commits (its changelog section lists the library-agnostic change);
4. `git ls-remote --tags origin 'v*'` is empty.

The push is pre-approved (amendment 1 D); RayX's switch (`rayx-released-libraries`) follows right after it (the compatibility trap). A failed item is reported to the owner, whose disposition it is; the push waits for it.
